      "def:minimum-cost-augmentation"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Let \\(M\\) be the union of \\(q_{\\max}\\) copies of the graphic matroid on \\(E\\cup E^+\\), and put\n\\[\nK=q_{\\max}(|V|-1),\\qquad r_E=r_{q_{\\max}}(E).\n\\]\nChoose a basis \\(I\\subseteq E\\) of the restriction \\(M|E\\), so \\(|I|=r_E\\).  The feasibility hypothesis says that \\(M|(E\\cup E^+)\\) has rank \\(K\\).  By the matroid basis-extension axiom, \\(I\\) extends using candidate elements to a basis \\(I\\cup F\\) of size \\(K\\).  Equivalently, the contraction by \\(I\\), after deletion of \\(E\\setminus I\\), has a basis \\(F\\subseteq E^+\\) of size\n\\[\n|F|=K-r_E=q_{\\max}(|V|-1)-r_{q_{\\max}}(E).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nThe candidate ground set is finite, so a minimum-weight such basis exists.\n\nNow let \\(F'\\subseteq E^+\\) be any saturating augmentation.  Again by basis extension, \\(I\\) extends to a basis of \\(M|(E\\cup F')\\) using a subset \\(F''\\subseteq F'\\), and (1) gives \\(|F''|=K-r_E\\).  If \\(F'\\setminus F''\\ne\\varnothing\\), strict positivity of every \\(c^+(e)\\) makes \\(F''\\) strictly cheaper.  Therefore every minimum-cost saturating augmentation contains no redundant element and has exactly the cardinality in (1).  This also proves the equivalence between minimum-cost saturation and the minimum-weight basis problem in the contracted union matroid.\n\nThe equality \\(r_{q_{\\max}}(E\\cup F_*)=K\\) means that \\(G+F_*\\) contains \\(q_{\\max}\\) edge-disjoint spanning trees.  Retaining any \\(q_x\\) of them and applying the exact generic rank theorem gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A_{P,x})=q_x(|V|-1)\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nfor almost every attainable row collection in every stratum.  For any compatible target population,\n\\[\nP(X=x,W=\\cdot\\mid S=0)\n=P(X=x\\mid S=0)\\,\\pi_{0,x}^{\\mathsf T}C_x\n\\in\\operatorname{row}(C_x).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nCommon-proxy factorization gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{row}(A_{P,x})\n\\subseteq\\operatorname{row}(I_{|V|-1}\\otimes C_x),\n\\]\nand both sides have dimension \\(q_x(|V|-1)\\) under (2), so the row spaces are equal.  Equation (3), node block by node block, therefore implies \\(\\operatorname{row}(R_0)\\subseteq\\operatorname{row}(A_P)\\), or \\(\\ker(A_P)\\subseteq\\ker(R_0)\\).  Exact nullity identifies all root contrasts for every compatible target mixture.  If feasibility fails at rank \\(K\\), no subset of the candidate menu can contain \\(q_{\\max}\\) disjoint spanning trees, and the same exact generic rank theorem rules out simultaneous saturation.",
    "justification": "The proposition gives one simultaneous multistratum design and proves that every positive-cost optimum uses exactly the union-rank deficit in new edges.",
    "gap": "Classical weighted matroid algorithms do not supply the causal row-space argument or the \\(q_{\\max}\\) heterogeneous-stratum interpretation.",
    "consumer": "NETMOTION planners can distinguish a rank-completing trial menu from one that is provably unable to saturate all strata.",
